course: language
lesson: 3
title: Verbs: doing words and being words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A verb tells you what happens or what is: in "The dog barked" the verb is barked, and every full English sentence has at least one verb.
explain: Not all verbs are busy: "The sky is blue" has the verb is, a being word; am, is, are, was and were are all forms of the verb be, the most common verb in English.
explain: Play charades: "swimming" is guessed in two seconds, but "think" is impossible to act, because it happens inside your head; thinking, knowing and believing are still verbs, just invisible ones.
explain: Verbs work in teams: in "I can swim", can is a helping (modal) verb and swim is the main verb; and the verb must match its doer, so it is "he goes" but "they go".
explain: English keeps making new verbs from nouns (to hoover, to google) and gives old verbs new slang meanings, like "she ate that" for "she did it brilliantly"; local verbs live on too, like Geordie "gan" for go.
fact: A verb is a doing or being word.
fact: A sentence is a group of words with a subject and a verb that makes complete sense; every full sentence has at least one verb.
fact: Be is the most common verb in English; its forms include am, is, are, was and were; have and seem are other verbs that show little action.
fact: Action verbs show actions you can see, like swim, jump and kick; mental verbs show thinking, knowing or feeling, like think, know and believe.
fact: Modal verbs like can, could, will, should and must help the main verb, which carries the main action.
fact: The verb must agree with its subject: one doer usually takes "goes" (he goes), more than one takes "go" (they go).
fact: In writing, "Ali and I go to the park" is the standard form of "Me and Ali go to the park".
fact: "Could have" said fast sounds like "could of", but the correct written forms are "could have" or "could've"; the same goes for should have and would have.
fact: A contraction squeezes two words into one with an apostrophe, like could've for could have.
fact: Brand names can become verbs: in British English "to hoover" means to vacuum, from the Hoover company; "to google" works the same way.
fact: In current slang, "ate" or "ate and left no crumbs" means did something brilliantly; the old meaning of eat still exists too.
fact: In the North East of England "howay" means come on and "gan" means go; "gan" comes from a very old English verb.
fact: In the Midlands and North of England "nesh" describes someone who feels the cold easily or is a bit soft.
exercise: What is the verb in "The dog barked"? | barked | dog | the | The dog
exercise: Yasmin says "is" can't be a verb in "The sky is blue". Why is she wrong? | Verbs can show being, and is is a form of be | Is is a noun that names the sky and the clouds | Is is only a verb in questions | Is is an adjective describing blue
exercise: Which is the most common verb in English? | be | go | have | say
exercise: Which of these is a mental verb? | believe | kick | jump | swim
exercise: In charades, why is "think" so hard to act out? | It happens in the mind, so you can't see it | It is not really a verb at all, just a word | It is a noun, not an action | It has too many letters
exercise: In "I can swim", which words are verbs? | Both can and swim | Only swim, because can is a noun | Only swim | Only I
exercise: What is a modal verb? | A helping verb like can, must or should | A verb that names a model or a toy figure | A verb used only in questions | The last verb in a sentence
exercise: Tom writes "Me and Ali goes to the park." What should "goes" be? | go | went goes | going | gone
exercise: Why does it need to change? | Two people are doing it, so the verb must agree with them | Goes is never a real word in written English, only in speech | Parks always take go | The sentence is in the future
exercise: Which form would a teacher expect in school writing? | Ali and I go to the park | Me and Ali goes to the park | Ali and me goes to the park | I and Ali goes to the park
exercise: Leo writes "I could of come". What should he write? | I could have come | I could off come | I could of came | I could has come
exercise: Why do people write "could of" by mistake? | "Could've" said fast sounds like "could of" | Teachers used to teach it as the correct form | Teachers used to teach it | It is the American spelling
exercise: Isla's mum texts "Hoover the stairs pls". What does she want? | The stairs vacuumed | A new Hoover bought | The stairs painted | The vacuum cleaner carried upstairs
exercise: Callum's cousin says a dancer "ate that, no crumbs". What does he mean? | The dancer did it brilliantly | The dancer ate a snack while dancing | The dancer was very hungry | The dancer dropped crumbs on stage
exercise: A lad from Newcastle shouts "Howay man, gan on, shoot!" What is he saying? | Come on, go on, shoot! | How are you, man, shoot later! | Stop it, man, don't shoot! | Hooray, the gang shot!
exercise: Gran says "Don't be nesh, put your coat on." What does nesh mean? | Feeling the cold easily, a bit soft | Very clever and quick at school work | Lazy about tidying up | Too noisy indoors
exercise: How many verbs are in "I think she can swim, but she is not brave"? | Four: think, can, swim, is | Two: think and swim | Three: think, swim, is | Five: think, can, swim, is, brave
exercise: In a made-up language, "zib" means run, "zibba" means ran and "mok" means jump. Following the same rule, what means jumped? | mokka | zibmok | mokzib | mokba
test: Your mate goes "a sentence with no verb is fine if it's well long." Is he right? | No, a full sentence needs at least one verb | Yes, length alone makes a sentence complete | Yes, if it has lots of nouns | No, it needs at least three verbs
test: Ben first says the verb in "The dog barked" is "dog". What question would help him? | Which word says what happened? | Which word is the longest? | Which word has a capital? | Which word is first?
test: A Gen Z cousin says "ngl 'ate' is ruined now, it doesn't mean food anymore." ("Ngl" means not gonna lie.) What's true? | The old meaning stays and the slang meaning is added | He's right, the word eat has lost its old meaning completely | The slang meaning was there first | "Ate" was never a verb
test: What would happen if verbs never agreed with their subjects? | Some sentences would be less clear about how many are doing it | Nothing at all, it would sound exactly the same and mean the same too | All verbs would end in -ing | Nobody could say "I"
test: Your cousin says "you can't google something, Google's a company, innit." What's the best reply? | Brand names often become verbs, like google and hoover | He's right, brand names can never be used as verbs in English | Only in America is googling allowed | To google means to buy a phone
test: In a video game, the hero only "does" things: run, jump, hit. A gamer says "thinking ain't a verb cos you can't see it." Is that right? | No, think is a mental verb | Yes, verbs must be visible | Yes, think is a noun | No, think is an adjective
test: Dylan says "In 'You must stop', there is only one verb." What's true? | Must is a helping verb and stop is the main verb | Must is a noun | You is the main verb | There is no verb at all
test: Mira from Derby says her pal from Bristol is "proper nesh" for wearing a scarf in May. What does she mean? | Her pal feels the cold very easily | Her pal is very fashionable and loves scarves | Her pal is very brave | Her pal is very angry
test: Your brother writes "We should of left earlier." What should it be? | We should have left earlier | We should off left earlier | We should of leaved earlier | We should has left earlier
test: Gran from Gateshead says "Howay, pet, we're gannin' yem." ("Yem" is home, "pet" is a friendly word like love.) What is she saying? | Come on, love, we're going home | Hurry, pet, we're getting a dog | Look, pet, we're gaining weight | Stop, love, we're staying here
test: Why is "be" so hard to spot as a verb? | It shows being, not action, and changes form: am, is, was | It is spelled differently in every English-speaking country | It only appears in questions | It is usually left out of sentences
exam: Identify the verb contained in the sentence "The dog barked". | barked | dog | the | The dog
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the word "is" in the sentence "The sky is blue"? | It is a form of the verb "be", denoting a state rather than an action | It is a noun denoting the atmosphere | It is an adjective qualifying the colour | It is not a word of any recognised class
exam: Ascertain the correct written form of the expression commonly heard as "could of come". | could have come | could of came | could off come | could has come
exam: Given a subject comprising two persons, select the response consistent with the rules of agreement in standard English. | Ali and I go to the park | Ali and I goes to the park | Me and Ali goes to the park | Ali and me goes to the park
